  <h1>Zimride is gone — how college students share rides in 2026</h1>
  <div class="meta">Updated July 2026</div>

  <p class="lede">Zimride was the original campus rideshare board — it's literally where Lyft came from. But the platform quietly wound down years ago, and the campus ride boards that replaced it are a patchwork of GroupMe chats and Facebook groups. If you're trying to get home for break without a car (or fill your car to cover gas), here's what actually works now.</p>

  <h2>What Zimride got right</h2>
  <p>Zimride understood two things: students take the <em>same long trips on the same dates</em> (breaks, long weekends, big games), and a car with four students splitting gas beats everyone driving — or paying for flights — alone. That demand didn't go anywhere. Only the platform did.</p>

  <h2>The alternatives, honestly compared</h2>
  <div class="scroll">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Safety / payment</th><th>The catch</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td class="win">Seat Sherpa</td><td>ID + driver's-license-verified drivers, payment held until a pickup code confirms the trip, two-way ratings, 18+ only</td><td>California &amp; Nevada routes for now</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Campus Facebook / GroupMe boards</td><td>None — you're Venmo-ing a stranger and hoping</td><td>Flaky no-shows, no recourse, boards die each graduation</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Craigslist rideshare</td><td>None</td><td>Same, with less of a community filter</td></tr>
      <tr><td>FlixBus / Amtrak</td><td>Commercial carriers</td><td>Fixed schedules, terminal-to-terminal, often slower door-to-door</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <h3>🎓 Built for exactly these trips</h3>
    <p>Seat Sherpa is a cost-sharing carpool marketplace: drivers already making the drive list their empty seats, and the price can only be a fair split of the trip's real cost — think gas money, not a fare. Popular student routes are already live:</p>
    <div class="routes">
      <a href="/routes/los-angeles-to-san-francisco">UCLA/USC → Bay Area</a>
      <a href="/routes/san-luis-obispo-to-los-angeles">Cal Poly SLO → LA</a>
      <a href="/routes/santa-barbara-to-los-angeles">UCSB → LA</a>
      <a href="/routes/san-francisco-to-sacramento">Berkeley → Sacramento/Davis</a>
      <a href="/routes/los-angeles-to-san-diego">LA → UCSD</a>
    </div>
    <a class="btn" href="/rides">Browse live rides</a>
  </div>

  <h2>Driving home anyway? Fill your seats.</h2>
  <p>The other half of Zimride was drivers. If you're a student <em>with</em> a car making the run home, three passengers at cost-split prices can cover your whole tank and then some — <a href="/drive">post your ride</a> and you keep 95% of every seat.</p>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <div class="faq"><h3>Is Zimride still around?</h3><p>No — it wound down years ago and isn't available at colleges anymore.</p></div>
  <div class="faq"><h3>What do students use instead of Zimride?</h3><p>Cost-sharing carpool apps like Seat Sherpa (verified + payment-protected), campus Facebook/GroupMe boards, and Craigslist — the informal boards work but offer no verification or recourse.</p></div>
  <div class="faq"><h3>How much does a rideshare home for break cost?</h3><p>A fair share of the driver's costs: roughly $32–$52.50 LA↔Bay Area, $24–$38 SLO↔LA, $16–$28 Santa Barbara↔LA, booking fee included.</p></div>

  <div class="callout" style="text-align:center">
    <h3>Going home for break?</h3>
    <p>Grab a seat with a verified driver already making the drive.</p>
    <a class="btn" href="/#get">Get the Seat Sherpa app</a>
  </div>

